    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d131">

                <pb facs="0220" n="118" xml:id="pg118"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 14911/7.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chinese Minister.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1908-11-16"><hi rendition="#italic">November 16,
                        1908</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 123.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your note of to-day’s date formally conveying to me the sad intelligence of
                    the demise of Her Imperial Majesty the Empress Dowager.</p>
                <p>Upon the receipt of the news of this additional national bereavement I desire to
                    express the profound sorrow of the Government and the people of the United
                    States.</p>
                <p>A telegram has been sent to the American legation at Peking instructing Minister
                    Rockhill to convey to His Imperial Majesty the Emperor and His Imperial Highness
                    Prince Ch’un, the Regent, the deep regret and sincere sympathy of the Government
                    and people of the United States at the great affliction sustained by the people
                    of the Chinese Empire.</p>
                <p>Accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Elihu Root.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
